[{:db/id 17592186119974 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aaa4309c-ee3c-55c4-881c-1b4771765495" "question-ea5b88c7-6c7f-53c0-965d-0668d1eb1c86"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119520 :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{15^2}\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nIn our case, since the number under the square root ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$) is already written as a square, we obtain the answer immediately: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{15^2} = 15\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119975 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5fe430ef-c20d-597d-9859-985f750b2fa1" "question-2b6108bf-af89-5508-a3bd-1a84295d0dad" "question-9b61ab50-fe75-5b5d-9dae-9c3fe9b50519" "question-2a2d2f86-1c36-54ef-a39e-becd7dbd3cec"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119521 :question/problem "Calculate $\\sqrt{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nTo work out $\\sqrt{36},$ we express $36$ as a perfect square: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6\\cdot 6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{6}^{2}} & = \\\\ 6 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119976 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e7d87357-9dbd-5804-b609-79909ab26f10" "question-ed622752-4bb3-5695-8b97-40b6d4099979"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119522 :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\geq 0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{a^2} = a.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $9$ and $64$ are both perfect squares, where\n\n$$\n9=3^2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n64 = 8^2.\n$$\n\nSo, to work out $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9}{64}},$ we express $\\dfrac{9}{64}$ as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{8\\cdot 8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119977 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97407b65-a5d7-539f-9f4c-1c8b734462e0" "question-5eb0d8b8-4879-5688-ac59-46c90d81a790"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119523 :question/problem "Which of the following square roots are real numbers?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{0}$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{1.5}$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{-4}$" :question/worked-solution "The square root of a negative number is not a real number, while the square root of a non-negative number is a real number.\n\nLet's look at each square root in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{0}$ is the square root of $0,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{0}$ is a real number. In fact, since $0 = 0^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{0} = \\sqrt{0^2} = 0.\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{1.5}$ is the square root of $1.5,$ which is a non-negative number. So $\\sqrt{1.5}$ is a real number.\n\n- $\\sqrt{-4}$ is the square root of $-4,$ which is a negative number. So $\\sqrt{-4}$ is not a real number.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-aaa4309c-ee3c-55c4-881c-1b4771765495" :question/id #uuid "aaa4309c-ee3c-55c4-881c-1b4771765495" :question/math-academy-id "q-178686" :question/problem "Expressed in its simplest form, $\\sqrt{{123}^{2}}=${{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{a}^{2}}=a.\n$$\n\nIn our case, since the number under the square root ($\\sqrt{}$) is already written as a square, we obtain the answer immediately:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{123}^{2}}=123\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c71e49a6-b7c6-5a0a-82a5-79c0e3067c67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c71e49a6-b7c6-5a0a-82a5-79c0e3067c67" :answer-field/id #uuid "c71e49a6-b7c6-5a0a-82a5-79c0e3067c67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0ecba3cc-fbbf-5659-a952